Firewall configureation

WAN rule

- IKE from coworker: action is set to Pass,	specify UDP protocol, for source is the public of my coworker, for destination IP we se set the IP of pfsense WAN interface, and for port we specify the 500
- NAT-T from coworker: action is set to Pass,	specify UDP protocol, for source is the public of my coworker, for destination IP we se set the IP of pfsense WAN interface, and for port we specify the 4500

Note: 
We need the Public IP in the L2L connection between my coworker because the two pfSense firewalls are at completely different physical locations separated by the public internet where in the previous Raspberry Pi tunnel, both endpoints were on the same home network with direct layer-2 reachability. No routing across the internet was needed. Below there're some reasons of why we need the public IP's for this connection:
  - Private IPs are not routable across the internet and each site's home router performs NAT, replacing the private source IP with the public IP on all outbound packets
  - we also need Port forwarding on each home router to directs inbound IKE/NAT-T traffic to the correct pfSense WAN interface

NAT-T (NAT Traversal) is a mechanism that allows IPsec to work through NAT devices such as home routers

IPsec rule that allow decrypted tunnel traffic to reach our DMZ

- allow coworker DMZ through tunnel: action is set to pass, protocol is set to any, source IP is set to coworker DMZ subnet,	destination IP is set to our DMZ subnet

Note: we apply this rule because pfSense drops all traffic by default even after it has been decrypted from the tunnel. This rule explicitly permits the decrypted traffic to flow from the IPsec virtual interface to the DMZ interface.

DMZ rules to allow our DMZ servers to send traffic into the tunnel

allow our DMZ to coworker DMZ: action is set to pass, protocol is set to any, source IP is set to our DMZ subnet,	destination IP is set to coworker DMZ subnet

Port forward in home router

Protocol	External Port	  Forward To	 Internal Port	 Purpose
UDP	       500	         pfsense WAN IP	    500	       IKE key exchange
UDP	       4500	         pfsense WAN IP	    4500	     NAT-T ESP encapsulation

UDP 500 is for IKE (Internet Key Exchange) and uses this for the initial negotiation between peers. Phase 1 and Phase 2 are negotiated here.
UDP 4500 is for NAT-T and uses this to encapsulate ESP packets in UDP so they can pass through NAT. Once NAT is detected, IKE also migrates to port 4500.

PKI generation in each pfsense

Each site creates its own CA and endpoint certificate independently. The certificates use the public IP as the CN and SAN because that is the identity each side presents across the internet.
On your pfSense — System > Cert Manager > CAs > Add:

Descriptive Name -> give a specific name
Method -> Create an internal Certificate Authority
Key type -> RSA
Key length -> 4096
Digest Algorithm -> sha256
Lifetime -> 3650 days 
Common Name	-> give a specific name

Note: longer key provides stronger long-term security. If someone records encrypted traffic now and the CA key is compromised years later, they could potentially decrypt it. 4096-bit makes this computationally infeasible for the foreseeable future.

Create the pfSense endpoint certificate — System > Cert Manager > Certificates > Add/Sign:

Method -> Create an internal Certificate
Descriptive Name -> give a specific name
Certificate Authority -> name of the cert from the CA that we created above
Key type -> RSA
Key length -> 4096
Digest Algorithm -> sha256
Lifetime -> 1825 days 
Common Name -> your public IP
Alternative Names	IP -> your public IP
Certificate Type -> Server Certificate

We put the public IP as CN and SAN  because during IKE_AUTH each pfSense presents its certificate and the remote peer verifies that the certificate's SAN matches the IP it connected to. 
Since the connection is established between public IPs, the SAN must contain the public IP. A mismatch results in AUTH_FAILED. Coworker's pfSense follows the same process with their values.

Now each site exports their CA certificate and sends it to the other. To export on our pfSense we go to System > Cert Manager > CAs > click Export CA next to the recently created CA cert and send the downloaded .crt file to my coworker.
To import coworker's CA on our pfSense we navigate to System > Cert Manager > CAs > Add:

Descriptive Name -> give specific name
Method -> Import an existing Certificate Authority
Trust Store -> enabled
Certificate data -> paste coworker's CA cert content

Note: Without Trust Store enabled pfSense stores the certificate but does not register it as a trusted root CA. 
This means pfSense cannot verify certificates signed by that CA during IKE_AUTH, resulting in AUTH_FAILED even if the certificate itself is valid.

Now we'll configure Phase 1 on our pfsense by navigate to VPN > IPsec > Tunnels > Add P1:

Key Exchange version -> IKEv2	(IKEv1 is deprecated)
Internet Protocol	-> IPv4
Interface	-> WAN (listen on WAN interface facing the internet)
Remote Gateway ->	Coworker's public IP that is the tunnel endpoint
Description

Authentication:

Authentication Method	-> Mutual RSA	(both sides authenticate with certificates)
My Identifier	IP Address ->	our public IP that matches your cert SAN
Peer Identifier	IP Address -> coworker's public IP that matches their cert SAN
My Certificate -> the server certificate that signed by our CA
Peer Certificate Authority -> coworker CA that used to verify coworker's certificate

Note: we use IP Address identifier format and not Distinguished Name because during testing with the Raspberry Pi tunnel I discovered that using Distinguished Name format (CN=x.x.x.x) caused pfSense to return "no matching peer config found" because StrongSwan presents a plain IP identity. 
IP Address format matches correctly on both implementations.

Encryption:

Encryption Algorithm -> AES 256 bits and AES-256-CBC for IKE control channel
Hash Algorithm -> SHA256 and HMAC-SHA256 for message integrity
DH Group -> 14 (2048 bit)	Diffie-Hellman Group 14 for key exchange
Lifetime -> 28800	before IKE SA renegotiation

Advanced:

Child SA Start Action	-> none (Responder Only) because our side waits and coworker initiates
NAT Traversal	-> force (both behind NAT so always use UDP 4500)
Dead Peer Detection	-> enabled
Delay -> 30	DPD check every 30 seconds
Max failures -> 3	declare dead after 3 failures

Note: we change to Force NAT-T instead of auto because auto would detect NAT during IKE_SA_INIT by comparing the source IP in the UDP header with the IP inside the IKE payload. 
If they differ NAT is detected. However forcing it is more reliable and avoids edge cases where auto-detection fails.

Phase 2 configuration by navigating to VPN > IPsec > Show Phase 2 Entries > Add P2:

Mode -> tunnel IPv4	(full tunnel entire packets encapsulated in ESP)
Local Network	Network -> our DMZ subnet
Remote Network	Network -> coworker's DMZ subnet
Description

Proposal:

Encryption -> AES 256 bits and AES-256-CBC
Hash -> SHA256 and HMAC-SHA256 for packet integrity
PFS Group	-> 14 (2048 bit) that used for fresh key per renegotiation
Lifetime -> 3600 before Phase 2 key rotation

Architecture explanation

Each site generates its own independent Certificate Authority. Neither site shares a common CA or private key material. Trust is established by each site importing the other's CA certificate.
